<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
Rails applications use the <code>ActionMailer</code> default URL options to build the absolute
links that are included in generated emails, such as password reset or account confirmation
links. If these options are set from request data, for example from the HTTP <code>Host</code>
or <code>X-Forwarded-Host</code> header, an attacker can choose the host of the links.
</p>
<p>
An attacker can, for instance, request a password reset for another user while supplying a
hostile <code>Host</code> header. The victim then receives a genuine email containing a link to
the attacker's server, and following the link leaks the secret reset token to the attacker.
Because the default URL options are global, the poisoned value may also affect emails sent
while handling other users' requests.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Configure the host, protocol and port used for links in emails statically, for example with
<code>config.action_mailer.default_url_options</code> in the environment configuration. If the
host must be derived from the request, validate it against a fixed allow-list of trusted hosts
first. Configuring <code>config.hosts</code> to reject requests with unexpected
<code>Host</code> headers provides additional protection.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, a controller callback copies the host of every request into the
<code>ActionMailer</code> default URL options:
</p>
<sample src="examples/bad.rb"/>
<p>
Instead, the host should be configured statically:
</p>
<sample src="examples/good.rb"/>
</example>

